I committed the fix as 7ed899e7 ("Fixes #1132"). Now yoyo ast --bogus and yoyo blame --bogus refuse with exit 2 and "Nothing ran", and a new table in src/verb_flags.rs records a decision for every shell verb. Build, clippy, fmt and all 34 test suites pass.

What changed
• One policy table: VERB_DASH_POLICY has a row for each of the 39 verbs yoyo dispatches. Each row is one of:
  • Refuse: the shared unknown-flag refusal (10 verbs).
  • OwnRefusal(reason): the verb's own parser already exits 2 before anything runs (27 verbs).
  • Passthrough(reason): the words go to something else on purpose (run and test).
• The table decides the routing: dispatch_sub.rs checks the table once, before it picks the verb's handler. The review and grep handlers' own calls became redundant and are gone, so the file shrank from 2637 to 2636 lines; I updated its size entry in tests/module_size.rs.
• Test that walks the dispatch table: every_known_subcommand_has_exactly_one_dash_policy_row fails in either direction: a new verb with no row, or a row for a verb that no longer exists. To check it can fail, I added a dummy verb, ran the test, and restored the file in one command. The test went red by name, then green again.
• Real-binary test: it reads the Refuse rows from the table's source rather than a typed list (the crate has no library target to import from), then runs <verb> --bogus for each. It asserts exit 2, "Nothing ran" and empty stdout. When I disabled the refusal, this test, the new ast/blame test and two existing review tests went red; restored, all 21 tests in the file pass.

The three verbs
• `ast`: I added its own flags, --lang and --in. ast --lnag now says "did you mean --lang?", even here where ast-grep isn't installed. Before, the "not installed" message hid the misread.
• `blame`: --bogus used to go straight to git blame, which errored with exit 1. Now it is refused. I also changed commands_git_review.rs, which wasn't on the task's file list, so git gets -- before the file name. That makes yoyo blame -- --weird blame a file actually named --weird; a test checks this.
• `test`: I left it as passthrough. In a Rust project, yoyo test --bogus ran cargo test --bogus. Forwarding words to the test runner is deliberate (#745) and documented, and yoyo test --release has to keep working.

Not covered: the table only covers the word right after the verb. One level down I found two misreads that still exit 0:
• yoyo goal set --bogus saves --bogus as the goal.
• yoyo skill search --bogus searches for --bogus.

Because this commit closes #1132, I filed those as #1136. I also posted the policy table and probe outputs on #1132. My script dropped the test row from that table, so I added it in a follow-up comment.

I added a sentence to docs/src/usage/commands.md covering the refusal, the -- escape and the two passthrough verbs, plus an entry in ARCHITECTURE.md.
